  <h2>Where AI blog drafts read flat</h2>
  <p>Blog drafts from an assistant often open with a line about "today's fast-paced world", promise to "delve into" the topic, and close each section with a tidy restatement. Readers learn to skim that pattern fast. Removing it makes the post shorter and puts your real points closer to the top.</p>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <blockquote><p><strong>Before:</strong> In today's digital age, email marketing plays a vital role in customer retention. Additionally, it serves as a cost-effective channel for a wide range of businesses.</p></blockquote>
  <blockquote><p><strong>After:</strong> Today, email marketing is key to customer retention. It is a cost-effective channel for all kinds of businesses.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>Your formatting stays put</h2>
  <p>The editor works one paragraph at a time. Headings, tables, code blocks and quoted passages are left exactly as they are, list numbering is locked, and links are locked so an anchor or URL can't change. You can paste Markdown straight from your CMS and paste the result straight back.</p>

  <h2>A publishing routine that works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Run the post at Balanced, then read the edit log rather than the whole text.</li>
    <li>Undo any edit that shifts a term your readers search for. Revert it with "This edit is wrong".</li>
    <li>Add what no tool can add: your own data, a screenshot, a customer quote or a mistake you made.</li>
    <li>Rewrite the introduction yourself. It sets the voice for everything after it.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Our <a href="/guides/edit-ai-blog-post">guide to editing an AI blog post</a> goes through this in more detail.</p>
  <h2>What about SEO?</h2>
  <p>Search engines reward pages that help the reader, and they don't reward a page for avoiding certain words. Cleaning up stock phrases won't lift a post by itself. What it does is make the post shorter and easier to read, which helps people stay and find your point.</p>
  <p>Two cautions when you edit a post that targets a search term:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Protect your keywords.</strong> If a rule swaps a word that's part of the phrase you want to rank for, revert that edit in the log. Headings are never changed, so a keyword in a heading is safe.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't publish the edit alone.</strong> A lightly edited AI draft is still a generic draft. Add first-hand detail before it goes live, as the routine above describes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Long posts are fine</h2>
  <p>You can paste up to 10,000 words at once, which covers almost any article. The edit still takes well under a second on a typical computer, because the work happens on your device and nothing is uploaded. Use the edit log to move through a long post quickly: it lists every change in order, so you don't have to reread the full text to see what happened.</p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <h3>Does it keep Markdown and links?</h3>
  <p>Yes. Markdown headings, lists, tables, code blocks and links are locked and come back exactly as you pasted them.</p>
  <h3>Will it change my product or brand names?</h3>
  <p>Capitalized names are locked before any rule runs. If a brand name is written in lower case, check the edit log for it.</p>
  <h3>Can I check a draft without editing it?</h3>
  <p>Yes. The <a href="/ai-phrase-checker">AI phrase checker</a> counts stock phrases, and the <a href="/readability-checker">readability checker</a> scores how easy the post is to read.</p>
